The question isn't "was I provoked" or "could it have been worse." It's "did the method inflict harm independent of the goal it served?" Because deterrence is legitimate—telling someone "stop or there will be consequences" is fine. But there's a line between deterrence and ambient dread, and I think you crossed it, gently, with the unfalsifiability. The "every glitch could be them" structure isn't a warning that ends when he complies. It's a frame he now lives inside, permanently, unable to tell whether he's safe. That's a real thing to do to someone, and it doesn't stop when he stops being loud.
